<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Benches Database</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdn.simplecss.org/simple.min.css">
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/styles.css">
</head>
<body>
    <header>
        <nav class="nav-links">
            <img src="https://pittsburghparks.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/09/PPC-Logo_Horizontal_Green_RGB.png" class="logo">
            <div style="display: flex; gap: 0rem;">
            <a href="/">Home</a>
            <a href="/entry">Add Bench</a>
            <a href="/maps">View Maps</a>
            <a href="/addmap">Add Map</a>
            <a class="current" href="/userguide">User Guide</a>
            </div>
        </nav>
    </header>

    <main>
        <h3>About</h3>
        <fieldset>
            This application was developed for the Pittsburgh Parks Conservancy to easily store commemorative benches in one
            database, with maps to visualize each park and its corresponding benches. Maps can be created from png files on which
            benches can be plotted and updated to visualize their availability for purchase.
        </fieldset>

        <h3>Benches</h3>
        <fieldset>
            Benches can easily be added into the database using the "Add Bench" page, where you will be prompted to add the main
            information regarding each bench. Once a bench is added to the database via this form, you can only access the bench through
            the homepage, where each bench is sorted by its corresponding park. By clicking on the bench on the homepage, you will be able
            to view all of its information. Note that information such as the Donor, Honoree, Payment Details, and Gift Date will not
            be displayed for benches that have not been purchased. Once you are viewing a singular bench's page, you can easily use the "Edit Bench"
            button to update any of the bench's information and save these changes. Benches can also be accessed through the "View Maps"
            page once they have already been plotted to a map. See below for how to create maps and plot benches.
        </fieldset>

        <h3>Map Features</h3>
        <fieldset>
            <legend>Adding New Maps</legend>
            Maps can be added on the "Add Map" page. Simply give the map a title, and upload an image of the blank map on which you would
            like to plot the benches. Each map must be uploaded as a png image. Once the image is uploaded, it will appear on the 
            'View Maps' page, where already existing benches can then be plotted onto the map. Each image will standardize to a maximum width 
            of 850px while maintaining the original proportions. If the image does not fill the entire width of the page, it 
            is likely too small and should be replaced by a larger image.
        </fieldset>
        <fieldset>
            <legend>Plotting Benches</legend>
            Benches must have already been added to the database using the 'Add Bench' page before they can be plotted on
            a map. Each map will have it's own form underneath it through which you can plot pre-existing benches. First, select
            from the dropdown menu which bench you would like to plot. Then, click on the map to select where you would like the
            bench to be placed. A red dot will preview the coordinates selected, so please adjust as needed before clicking the
            "Add" button to finalize. Benches will appear as a circle containing the bench's corresponding number within its park. 
            If the bench  is unavailable for purchase, it will show with a red X through it. When benches are purchased and updated 
            in the app, the maps will automatically reflect this. Each bench can be plotted to exactly 1 map, and will appear only 
            on the map it was most recently plotted to. If a map is deleted, all corresponding benches will appear just the same on
            the homepage, but will no longer have a corresponding map.
        </fieldset>

        <h3>The Codebase</h3>
        <fieldset>
            This application was built using Node.js and Express, and runs on a SQLite database. There is a main 'server.js' file which
            sets up the configuration for the application, and establishes different file routes for certain commands, such as adding and
            editing benches, adding and editing maps, or navigating to different pages. Each of these functions comes with its own .js file
            located in the 'routes' folder. These create the REST APIs for each possible endpoint which can affect the database. They tell
            the application which HTML files to load and what effect to have on the database based on the command that is given. Each button
            on every page has a corresponding path which the backend specifies through these route files, telling the application which data 
            to create, read, update or delete, and then which page to load next as a response.
        </fieldset>
    </main>

    <footer>
        <small>Pittsburgh Parks</small>
    </footer>
</body>
</html>